WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.251
A lunar rover team must decide which cabin materials still need fire-test evidence. NASA records exist, but each was tested under its own conditions.

2
00:00:10.259 --> 00:00:22.550
Load the illustrative lunar rover BOM. Each line is matched to candidate NASA records. All 45 records are drafts and 0 are verified, and the app says so.

3
00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:34.657
Window panel, record PSI25-B1: requested conditions beside what the source tested, with citation and page locator. Unknown stays unknown, never zero.

4
00:00:34.661 --> 00:00:39.891
Set the microgravity reference (0 g) and pin it as the baseline.

5
00:00:39.895 --> 00:00:45.575
Change one assumption: lunar gravity, 0.16 g (an estimate; no direct lunar data).

6
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:53.940
REDLINE lists exactly which records stopped matching and why. Fewer gaps would not mean lower fire risk.

7
00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:59.782
Each gap becomes an ordered research question naming the field and the records to check.

8
00:00:59.786 --> 00:01:09.998
Export the reviewer brief as Markdown and JSON: conditions, citations, review states, what changed, next questions.

9
00:01:10.002 --> 00:01:18.844
This is the exported file itself, built from data rather than generated prose. It is not a safety assessment.

10
00:01:19.093 --> 00:01:24.473
A reviewer reopens the downloaded JSON brief and sees the same reasoning.

11
00:01:24.498 --> 00:01:35.468
Illustrative scenario, draft sources, no predictions. REDLINE organises evidence review; engineers and safety reviewers decide.
